int x; // declared, uninitialised — value is GARBAGE
int y = 5; // copy-initialisation
int z(7); // direct initialisation (constructor-style)
int w{9}; // braced (uniform / list) initialisation — RECOMMENDED
double pi = 3.14159;
char letter = 'A';
bool active = true;
std::string name = "Ada"; // requires #include <string>

const int MAX = 100;
MAX = 200; // ERROR — cannot modify const
Once declared const, the variable cannot be reassigned. Use const aggressively — it documents intent and lets the compiler optimise.

void print(const std::string& s) { // pass by const ref — no copy, can't modify
std::cout << s << "\n";
}
This is the way to pass large objects to functions in C++: by const &. It avoids copies but guarantees the function can't modify the argument.
We'll cover pointers (int*) in detail in Post 02. References are usually the cleaner choice.

When mixing types, the compiler promotes the smaller/narrower to the wider:
int i = 5;
double d = 2.0;
auto r = i + d; // r is double, value 7.0
Signed + unsigned of the same size → unsigned. Mixing signed and unsigned is a notorious bug source:
unsigned int u = 1;
int s = -1;
if (s < u) { ... } // SURPRISE: s is converted to unsigned (huge), so condition is FALSE
Use signed types unless you have a specific reason for unsigned.

if (x > 0) {
std::cout << "positive";
} else if (x == 0) {
std::cout << "zero";
} else {
std::cout << "negative";
}
Braces are optional for single-statement bodies, but always use them — prevents the dangling-statement bug.

if (auto it = m.find(key); it != m.end()) {
// use it->second
}
The variable it is scoped to the if/else. Keeps tiny helpers out of the surrounding scope.

while (n > 0) {
process(n);
--n;
}
do {
/* runs at least once */
} while (still_running());

for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
std::cout << i << " ";
}
Three parts: init, condition, increment.

std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
for (int x : v) { // copy each element
std::cout << x << " ";
}
for (const auto& x : v) { // const reference — no copy, no mutation
std::cout << x << " ";
}
for (auto& x : v) { // mutable reference — can modify
x *= 2;
}
const auto& is the idiomatic default. Use plain auto only for small types like int/double.

switch (cmd) {
case 1:
do_one();
break;
case 2:
case 3:
do_two_or_three();
break;
default:
do_other();
}
Don't forget break — without it, execution falls through to the next case. (Sometimes useful; usually a bug.)
C++17 added [[fallthrough]]; to mark intentional fall-through:
case 1:
foo();
[[fallthrough]];
case 2:
bar();
break;

int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
void greet(const std::string& name) {
std::cout << "Hello, " << name << "\n";
}
int main() {
std::cout << add(3, 4) << "\n"; // 7
greet("Ada");
}

void by_value(int x) { x = 999; }
void by_reference(int& x) { x = 999; }
void by_const_ref(const int& x) { /* x = 999; // ERROR */ }
int n = 5;
by_value(n); // n is still 5
by_reference(n); // n is now 999
Default rule:
Small types (int, double, pointers) → pass by value.
Large types (std::string, containers, your own classes) → pass by const & unless you need to mutate.

int power(int base, int exp = 2) {
int r = 1;
for (int i = 0; i < exp; ++i) r *= base;
return r;
}
power(5); // 25
power(5, 3); // 125
Defaults must be on trailing parameters.

Print 1–100; for multiples of 3 print "Fizz", 5 → "Buzz", both → "FizzBuzz".
Solution
#include <iostream>
int main() {
for (int i = 1; i <= 100; ++i) {
if (i % 15 == 0) std::cout << "FizzBuzz";
else if (i % 3 == 0) std::cout << "Fizz";
else if (i % 5 == 0) std::cout << "Buzz";
else std::cout << i;
std::cout << "\n";
}
}

Read a positive integer, print the sum of its digits.
Solution
#include <iostream>
int main() {
long long n;
std::cin >> n;
long long sum = 0;
while (n > 0) {
sum += n % 10;
n /= 10;
}
std::cout << sum << "\n";
}

Use constexpr for the conversion factor. Read Celsius, print Fahrenheit.
Solution
#include <iostream>
constexpr double NINE_FIFTHS = 9.0 / 5.0;
constexpr double OFFSET = 32.0;
int main() {
double c;
std::cin >> c;
std::cout << c << "C = " << (c * NINE_FIFTHS + OFFSET) << "F\n";
}

Write void swap_ints(int& a, int& b) that exchanges two ints. Verify from main.
Solution
#include <iostream>
void swap_ints(int& a, int& b) {
int tmp = a;
a = b;
b = tmp;
}
int main() {
int x = 1, y = 2;
swap_ints(x, y);
std::cout << x << " " << y << "\n"; // 2 1
}

